ose timelines provided by the  regulator and respond appropriately  key here is you need to make sure you're  staffed appropriately to do so when  you're in the middle of submitting  a submission  and  you have a  complicated product it's probably going  to take more staff to complete that  submission than if you have  a run-of-the-mill sort of simple  product that you're bringing to market  and  you need to take a really close look at  what your requirements are going to be  to understand how you need to staff up  to meet the needs of the regulators  this allows you as a manufacturer to  understand well  you know do i if i want to come to  market it you know  next year what's it going to take to get  there how might i go quicker or  strategically if i need to slow down how  do i plan for that  you're going to need staff that help  with this  if you don't have staff internally you  need to reach out to external  contractors to help you develop that  strategy but the key here is make sure  up front that you know how you're going  to interact with those regulators and  make sure that you're staffed with  people that know how to do that whether  internally or externally  and remember those timelines  are literally set in stone so if you  want to  respond as quickly as you can to the  regulators you've got to know what their  expected timelines are  so here's a  basic  piece of background on the us and the eu  and the key is  each of the  regulating bodies for the fda in the  united states or notified bodies in the  eu  play a critical role in protecting the  public's health  the united states fda  looks at safety and effectiveness  and they decide is that test meeting um  those specifications is the quality of  the design and manufacture of the  diagnostic test appropriate to move  forward  in a safe and effective manner that's  what fda looks like  in addition in the  in the us while fda looks at safety and  and  effectiveness  medicare  when it comes to diagnostics  looks at the quality of the clinical  laboratories in the clinical testing  process  so there's there's two pieces in the u.s  if you're bringing a diagnostic to  market  one is fda which deals with the safety  and effectiveness and  for shorthand what we would say the  approval of that product but cms also  then regulates the quality of the  clinical laboratory so this is what we  talked about when we say clia or clia  waived so you need to understand that if  you're bringing a diagnostic to the us  market you need to make sure that you  understand the fda process and the cms  process  flipping over to the eu  all diagnostic tests are required to  undergo a conformity assessment  and that's where the manufacturer  demonstrates that the product complies  with the requirements set  out in the application  in the applicable rather legislation  and your notified body  really assesses the conformity of those  products  so at the end of the process in the eu  you are given a ce mark and that  demonstrates that you have  satisfied the applicable eu requirements  so this is just the u.s and the eu there  are many other geos i know you're all  working in  but these are two that i find are very  useful for people wanting to learn more  about diagnostics because they're there  where there are very  um  there are slight differences in how each  of those geos move those diagnostics  forward  so focusing first on notified bodies in  ibdr  i would be remiss if i didn't say that  in europe  the ibd regulations that are coming into  play are really the biggest change in  the ibd space that we've seen in the  last  decade or more  and the key here is it relies on  notified bodies  for those not familiar a notified body  is  really that key pillar in the medical  technology regulatory system in in the  eu they really provide the the review  in consultation with the manufacturer on  making sure that they meet  the  um requirements  as you will see on the right slide right  side of this slide  prior to the ibdr regulations going into  place  about 20 of um  ce mark  met the requirements based on working  with a notified body and having a  conformity assessment  but the other 80 percent you didn't need  the notified body conformity assessment  the shift that is happening now and goes  finally into place in may of 2022  is that 80  will be certified with notified body  conformity assessment so if you can see  we've gone from 20  percent  of the ibd on the market in europe  needing notified body conformity  assessment to 80  now needing certification with notified  body conformity assessment  this is a big lift this is a big change  for those of you newer to the  ivd space  this has been a significant lift for any  manufacturer in this space working in  europe  so i've added this topic in here just as  a reminder to those of you that are  deep in this space but also those of you  that are newer to this space that as you  move towards diagnostics in the eu there  are some very significant very  significant regulatory changes  this is just a quick slide on the  timeline so again  many of you know this  enforcement for ivdr begins may 26 2022  and then there will be a period of time  before  the previous what were referred to as  ivdd certificates expire  and then there's a period of time before  those devices have to be off the eu  market so again 2022  is when that enforcement begins for ibdr  and then by  um  late 2024 early 2025 or when those  those uh diagnostics that do not meet  the new requirements must  begin to be removed from the market  so when you look at the new regulation  of diagnostics in europe the big key  here is that  if you're bringing an ibd  to the european market there's just a  different  level of  restriction now  and  you need to make sure that you're  familiar with these dates and  understanding that once fully  implemented  ibd compliance rules ensure almost all  of the ibds entering the european market  will be subject to that notified body  review remember you saw previously 20  now 80  that's how you'll move to ce mark  i want to switch for a minute and look  at the us  so many have asked about emergency use  authorization and public health  emergencies and what that means  as we're moving diagnostics forward so  in the united states we have emergency  use authorization or what we call eua  and  eua authority allows fda to help  strengthen the nation's public health  protections  there is a statute that allows this to  happen  and it allows the secretary of hhs to  declare an emergency use authorization  to be appropriate  to date when we look at the pandemic  that we're in there have been about 377  tests related to covet 19 that currently  have  eua  that does not include the numbers that  are in review right now with the fda  looking for eua  should you want more information  about what tests have been approved  under eua and in general more  information about testing i find that  the arizona state university testing  commons  run by maura asthma tends to be very  helpful i've put the link down here and  referenced for you so you can look at  that site but they have been pulling  data globally  and focusing on what tests are coming  through again specific to coven 19.  so  eua  again if you follow this stepwise from  step one to step six  we start with confirming that there's a  public health emergency  then once you have confirmed there's a  public health emergency there is a  formal declaration of that emergency  again these steps are important in the  us because it's how we're able to move  to eua  once you have that declaration of  emergency as you know you can start  developing your product and filing for  eua  and so that pre-eua submission is really  important because it's a piece  in the in the framework where you can  actually have that pre-meeting so that's  the pre-meeting that i talked about  in a normal frame it's a pre-submission  with a pre-eua submission meeting you  can actually talk about that product  with the fda get feedback from them  understand what else they need for your  filing and then  rapidly move to a more complete  submission  step four is that submission of the eua  step five is that approval or rejection  of the eua and again you'll be having  communication with the fda through this  whole process at the appropriate  timeline  and then  step six is eua termination so at some  point in the u.s  we will be out of this public health  emergency  and once  the u.s government determines that we  are no longer in a public health  emergency  they  they terminate the emergency they end  the emergency  and then those emergency use  authorizations  go away  and so the key for those of you  interested in filing diagnostics in the  united states and interested in using  the eua pathway you need to understand  that at some point  that expires and you need to transition  your diagnostic  to the normal regulatory pathway  if  you are intending to keep that in in the  market after a pandemic end  so again just a reminder on eua eua is a  temporary  approval if you think of it that way it  is for a defined period of time that  period of time being while there is a  public health emergency that has been  declared by the us government  once that public health emergency is  over  there is a a period of time in the eu  start to  um they terminate so you  as someone bringing forward diagnostics  to the in the us if you're bringing it  under eua need to understand when that  termination happens and if you're  intending to keep it on market how you  would convert it  in the eu there's also a way to declare  public health emergency for those of you  there you you know this well  and  in the case of  an emergency relating to health and  safety the  eu commission and member states will  grant access to the eu market for  devices that  have not followed the normal conformity  assessment routes  that is what's going on now  it is a temporary authorization of a  diagnostic test again under this public  health emergency for a limited period of  time  to date  there are about 748 tests related to  cobit 19 that have a ce mark  again this information is from  the testing commons at arizona state  university  so as i mentioned when i was talking  about eua what happens after this  remember  in any geo that you're in if you're  filing during  a public health emergency and there is  an expedited  approval process that you went through  eua or other you really need to make  sure you understand the regulatory  process to convert from that emergency  use  to what is the normal regulatory process  there's a lot of time that is involved  with this there are  generally more data that need to be  collected  and  you need to make sure that you're  preparing for that  so right now for many of us that have  filed  diagnostic tests under eua or other  public health emergency provisions in  other gos  many of us have to think about well if  we're going to keep this product on  market what are we going to do next so  again conversations with your regulating  authority  understanding how that process works are  essential for you being able to move  forward in that market  so protecting public health  happens when we bring needed tests to  market  so you've heard me talk a bit about  being in pandemic and the emergency use  authorization or other emergency use  processes you can go through to bring a  test to market but the key here when  responding to public health emergency in  this case a pandemic is you really need  to think globally  and you also then need to act locally  first  and the key here is you then need to  share those best practices as we try to  contain and eliminate the disease or  react to that  so whether in this case it's covid which  we are very all very deeply involved in  all of us are wrestling with but think  prior to cobit think about ebola  there were emergency authorization  provisions around ebola as well  and the key there was  we're responding to an outbreak we want  to make sure we can get the needed tests  there  and then we need to figure out  are the tests that are needed  in one region the same as in another  so again sharing those best practices in  order to contain  and eliminate disease is really  important and understanding how you're  going to protect public health is also  important so all of you that are  bringing diagnostics to market or all of  you that are trying to figure out how  best to leverage those diagnostics there  is going to be a component of what you  do that is very local  it is important to understand the needs  of each individual country  and that current public health framework  and then link those responses  and the key again there is you're going  to have different requirements in  different countries from a regulatory  pathway you already know that we've been  through that earlier in these slides  but the key also is  where you can leverage the same  technology globally it's a lot easier to  distribute your product but in some  cases there are going to be different  requirements or different disease  patterns that you have to meet  potentially with different tests so  understanding that up front  understanding what the public health  frameworks are within which you're going  to work and understanding how to match  your diagnostic that you're interested  in  to what that framework is will be  essential for your campaign forward  so a couple of questions  that that i always tell people to ask  for when bringing a product to market  especially in a public health emergency  number one who's doing the financing so  how are these tests paid for how are  these tests developed is there are there  any government funds to help develop  those tests these are important  we're in a pandemic there's been a lot  of funding around test development and  test deployment same with vaccine  development vaccine deployment  in a non-pandemic time when there is a  serious outbreak like take ebola  there is an important question of how is  that financing working and how does that  impact the regulatory or payment pathway  you're moving forward in in addition  what resources are available to  diagnostic manufacturers to ramp up  development quickly again where in  covert there's been more resources  available than i think any of us have  ever seen in the past but when you think  about outbreaks again using ebola as an  example  what resources are available both  regulatory advising resources meaning  are the regulators coming to the  diagnostic community and having  open forum to discuss the regulatory  pathways forward for this specific  disease type to discuss the the  confusion around any communication with  the regulatory pathway and to clarify  that regulatory pathway so understanding  those resources is important and then  finally how are those tests being paid  for again how development is financed is  one thing but then also depending on  what geo you're in understanding how  those tests are paid for is very  important as you're developing a  diagnostic test for the market  both within a pandemic and when you're  not an appendix  so other factors that i think are  important to consider in and out of  pandemic are  diversity and diversifying  so different populations responded  differently to coban 19. so if you take  that as your example  different populations  fared worse if they got coveted than  others different populations had testing  available to them  and so understanding the different  testing modalities and solutions needed  depending on what geo you're in are  important but even within a geo  you may have underrepresented  communities whether impoverished  communities underrepresented minority  communities people that bottom line do  not have access  to the products that you're producing  and you need to ask yourself the  question why is it  a system  issue meaning you've developed the  product but you can't get it to all the  people that need it well how do you fix  that so we don't end up with the haves  and the have-nots how do we  decide as manufacturers to meet those  needs of a diverse population  and how do we make sure that our  products are getting  to where they are needed everywhere  regardless  of those obstacles and  barriers in addition  when i talk about diversify i also mean  what are the diseases that you're  looking at to develop diagnostics around  you know post pandemic there is a need  for tests in a variety of spaces  especially in rare diseases and for  distinct groups  so sometimes there are differences  by  what your gender is by what your race is  to how you'll respond to  a disease process  some of it is related to  whether  you're a a group that is in the majority  or the minority other times it's related  to it just simply is a rare disease  and so as you're thinking about meeting  the needs of a very diverse community  where is your focus  on diseases that may have smaller  numbers of people but still people that  are in need of your tests  how do you think about how you're going  to develop your future diagnostics to  meet those needs  in addition  having diverse populations in your  clinical trials is essential we have  seen many efforts around the globe to  diversify clinical trials and i think  it's really important especially in the  time that we're in  in addition to diverse populations and  clinical trials  you really on need to look at real world  evidence development  so  we are  we are very used to randomized  controlled trials and understanding how  those are tightly controlled and how to  bring products to market using a very  well done randomized control trial  but what we're also seeing the market  move to and the regulators accepting  more is the notion that  that randomized control trial is really  important  but  how might we leverage real-world  evidence to move things forward  where we may not have an rct ready  and so understanding that there's a  combination of randomized control trial  and real-world evidence playing together  and that regulators are looking at both  is an important concept  in the us  there's a few different places we're  seeing this  pcori  which is one of the groups that's  looking at patient-centered outcomes is  looking at how you can leverage  real-world evidence  you have the medical device and  innovation consortium looking at  how do we put a framework around real  world evidence so that  regulators globally might be able to use  it  in a fashion that helps move products  forward in the market  and all the other geos have something  around real world evidence and the key  here is  know which geo you're in know which  regulators are looking at real world  evidence and be prepared for that coming  if you're not already there  finally when i look at personalized  medicine i look at it to say you know  how does this provide an understanding  of the community disease risk  so  if you take covid for example when we  were doing covet tests we saw  different distribution of the people  that were getting coveted in a city  in a state in a country in a region  and we saw some distinct patterns  that  pointed to  a  really a  a difference in who had access to  testing and then a difference in what  that outcome was so again looking at the  social determinants of health and  looking to limit disparities is  important and i think personalized  medicine plays a big role in that so for  those of you  listening who are in the diagnostic  space that are very focused on  personalized medicine i think it's a  really important place to be  finally on this it's really also about  how do we prepare for the next pandemic  better yet how do we prevent it and i  think diagnostic tests play a big role  in that  so some key takeaways you need to know  your regulators you need to know what  pathways are you need to know  how you can interact with them  appropriately  and if you don't know these things or  you're not staffed up  adequately to meet those needs you  really need to find external help  you need to be interacting with those  regulators before there's a critical  issue or an emergency that communication  is is key and understanding how you  access them is important there's  different groups  that have  very  good advice around this there's rats in  the u.s does a good job of this  as well as the trade associations here  and in europe  finally as you heard me say when i  opened vaccines will help us end covid19  please get vaccinated when you have a  chance wherever you are in the world  public health plays a key  role in keeping the world safe  diagnostics play a key role  in public health  all of you working in the diagnostic  space or thinking about working in the  diagnostic space or working adjacent to  the diagnostic space  have  an  a moment where you can say  you know what i'm going to make a  difference here i'm going to look at my  diagnostics that i'm developing i'm  going to look to see how this helps with  public health and at the end of the day  you're going to help build a society you  want to live in by keeping public health  at the forefront  so again  if you have a vaccine offered to you  please get the shot  until then if you're in an area where  there is not  enough  vaccine offered please stay safe keep  that mask on wash your hands  be kind and again thank you for your  time i appreciate your attention and  happy to answer any questions  thank you dr fine glass for that  outstanding presentation we will now  move into the live q a portion of the  presentation as a reminder please submit  your questions via the q a box  dr fine glass our first question is how  sophisticated do my questions have to be  when speaking to regulators  the questions do not need to be  complicated or technical  it's really important to establish that  strong foundational relationship with  the regulating agency